questions.prompt:
A retry configuration must start with the original settings, then allow independent changes.

```csharp
public sealed class Limits
{
    public int Timeout;
}

public struct RunConfig
{
    public int Attempts;
    public Limits Limits;
}

var original = new RunConfig
{
    Attempts = 2,
    Limits = new Limits { Timeout = 30 }
};

var retry = original; // Replace this line.
retry.Attempts = 5;
retry.Limits.Timeout = 60;
```

Replace the marked line so that `retry` initially contains the same settings, but the final two statements leave `original.Attempts` at `2` and `original.Limits.Timeout` at `30`. Do not change the type declarations.

Explain why the original assignment already isolates changes to `Attempts`, but fails to isolate changes to `Timeout`.

questions.model:
```csharp
var retry = new RunConfig
{
    Attempts = original.Attempts,
    Limits = new Limits
    {
        Timeout = original.Limits.Timeout
    }
};
```

`RunConfig` is a struct, so assignment copies each field. The copied `Attempts` field holds its own integer value; changing it does not change `original.Attempts`.

`Limits` is a class. Copying that field copies its object reference, so the original assignment leaves both configurations pointing to the same `Limits` instance. Changing its `Timeout` is visible through both configurations. The replacement creates a separate instance with the same initial timeout, allowing independent changes.
